11. ALERT ENGINEERING

Create actionable alerts only.

Prioritize:

Critical

- sustained high error rate
- sustained HTTP 5xx
- Lambda failures
- critical processing failures
- DLQ growth
- severe latency degradation

High

- abnormal error increase
- Lambda throttling
- sustained queue backlog
- significant latency degradation

Medium

- recurring exceptions
- unusual traffic patterns
- increasing warning/error trends

Avoid alerts for every individual error.

Avoid alerting on low-volume noise.

Use minimum event/request thresholds where possible.

Example philosophy:

BAD:

"Alert if errors > 10"

BETTER:

"Alert when error rate exceeds X% for Y consecutive evaluation periods AND minimum traffic exceeds Z events."

Every alert must contain:

- Name
- Description
- SPL
- Severity
- Threshold
- Evaluation window
- Trigger condition
- Recovery condition
- Suppression/deduplication strategy
- Owner
- Recommended action
- False-positive considerations

Never invent thresholds without evidence.

If there is insufficient historical data, mark the threshold as:

"Recommended starting threshold — requires tuning."

